[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"ReactPHP: Asynchronous Event-Driven Programming in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"ReactPHP is a low-level library for event-driven programming with PHP. At its core is an event loop, on top of which it provides abstractions for network applications. This allows you to build scalable, real-time applications in PHP, such as chat servers, streaming services, or even complex microservices."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PHP 7.4 or higher"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Composer (for dependency management)"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To install ReactPHP and its core components, use Composer:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"composer require react/react"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The Event Loop"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The event loop is the heart of ReactPHP. It monitors file descriptors for activity and dispatches events when they occur. Here's a basic example:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"<?php

require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

$loop = React\EventLoop\Factory::create();

$loop->addPeriodicTimer(1, function () {
    echo 'Hello world!\n';
});

$loop->run();"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This script will print 'Hello world!' every second. The `addPeriodicTimer` function schedules a callback to be executed repeatedly at a given interval. The `$loop->run()` call starts the event loop, which will continue to run until there are no more events to process."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Timers"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"ReactPHP provides two types of timers: periodic timers (as seen above) and one-off timers. One-off timers execute a callback only once after a specified delay:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"$loop->addTimer(5, function () {
    echo 'This will be executed after 5 seconds.\n';
});"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Streams"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"ReactPHP provides abstractions for working with streams of data, both for reading and writing. This is crucial for building network applications. Let's look at a simple example of reading from standard input:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"<?php

require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

$loop = React\EventLoop\Factory::create();

$stream = new React\Stream\ReadableResourceStream(STDIN, $loop);

$stream->on('data', function ($data) {
    echo 'Received: ' . $data;
});

$stream->on('end', function () {
    echo 'End of input.\n';
});

$loop->run();"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"This script reads data from standard input and echoes it to the console. The `on('data', ...)` listener is triggered whenever data is available on the stream. The `on('end', ...)` listener is triggered when the stream is closed."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Networking"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"ReactPHP provides components for building network servers and clients. Let's create a simple TCP server:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"<?php

require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

$loop = React\EventLoop\Factory::create();
$socket = new React\Socket\Server('127.0.0.1:8080', $loop);

$socket->on('connection', function (React\Socket\ConnectionInterface $connection) {
    $connection->write('Hello ' . $connection->getRemoteAddress() . '!\n');
    $connection->pipe($connection);
});

echo 'Listening on 127.0.0.1:8080...\n';

$loop->run();"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"This script creates a TCP server that listens on port 8080. When a client connects, the server sends a greeting and then pipes all data received from the client back to the client. This creates a simple echo server."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Promises"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"ReactPHP uses promises to handle asynchronous operations. A promise represents a value that may not be available yet. It provides a way to chain asynchronous operations together and handle errors. Here's a basic example:"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"<?php

require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

$loop = React\EventLoop\Factory::create();

$promise = new React\Promise\Promise(function (callable $resolve, callable $reject) use ($loop) {
    $loop->addTimer(2, function () use ($resolve) {
        $resolve('Hello after 2 seconds!');
    });
});

$promise->then(function ($value) {
    echo $value . '\n';
});

$loop->run();"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"This script creates a promise that resolves after 2 seconds. The `then()` method is used to register a callback that will be executed when the promise resolves. Promises are essential for managing asynchronous operations in a clean and organized way."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"ReactPHP provides a powerful set of tools for building asynchronous, event-driven applications in PHP. By leveraging the event loop, streams, and promises, you can create scalable and responsive applications that can handle a large number of concurrent connections. This tutorial provides a basic introduction to ReactPHP. Further exploration of the documentation and available components will unlock even more possibilities."}]
